re Segment A and Segment B equal in length?\", \"reasoning\": \"F10 names a ruler and gives marks on it (0 cm start, centimeter ends) in the question text.\"}}, \"failing_families\": [\"F05\", \"F16\", \"F17\", \"F19\", \"F21\"], \"failing_templates\": [\"A segment starting at {v} cm and ending at {v2} cm\", \"Start at the {start} mark and count on {length}: {start} + {length} = {answer}.\", \"Starting at the 0 mark, count {length} {count_noun}: Segment B ends at the {answer} mark.\", \"{val_a} and {val_b} are {v}, so the answer is {answer}.\", \"{member}\"], \"overall\": \"FAIL\", \"reasoning\": \"F21 (template {member}): the member list includes a choice containing the phrase 'set square', which is not in cumulative_vocab or NOT_YET_KNOWN, so it is vocabulary outside the lc's gates. F19: slot v (domain: the two equality verdicts, not declared derived from val_a, val_b or answer) and slot answer (No or Yes, declared derived from answer) are independent, so the hint can state that unequal values are the same, or equal values are not the same, with answer Yes. The sentence is false for those combinations. F17: length (2 to 9) and answer (printed 2 to 9) are independent with no derivation declared, so the hint can state a count from the 0 mark that ends at a mark other than the counted length. F16: start is computed from answer (3 to 14) minus length (2 to 9), whose image includes values below 0; the hint then prints a negative mark that does not exist on a grade 3 ruler. F05: v (2 to 6) and v2 (5 to 16) are independent, so the distractor's length can equal Segment A's length, making a distractor a correct answer. F02, F03, F04, F10, F12, F13, F14, F20 and the hint families F06, F07, F08, F09, F15, F18 were checked and not failed; F01 prints the single gated word centimeter (cm), and count-noun grammar such as a plural numeral before centimeter (cm) was noted but not treated as a content fault.\"}",
